WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.399
Congratulations!

00:00:01.399 --> 00:00:05.930
You just kicked off a week of R&amp;R at a tropical
paradise!

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:11.089
And best of all, the travel isn’t costing
you a dime because you used your FLIGHT REWARDS

00:00:11.089 --> 00:00:12.089
CARD!

00:00:12.089 --> 00:00:14.589
Feels good to get something for nothing, doesn’t
it?

00:00:14.589 --> 00:00:16.230
But air travel isn’t cheap.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.310
And credit card companies aren’t known for
their charity--or stupidity.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.880
So, what’s the catch?

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.550
You might be surprised to learn that that
“free” flight you just booked might be

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:36.730
the most expensive one you’ve ever taken.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:42.370
Starting with the Diner’s Club Card in 1950,
Americans instantly fell in love with plastic.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:46.910
Within just a generation or two, credit cards
went from being a perk for wealthy world travelers

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.200
to a practical necessity used by over 70%
of U.S. households.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.210
And what’s not to like?

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:57.050
As long as you pay your bills on time, it’s
basically an interest-free loan!

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:03.551
And then there’s the Rewards programs: cash
back, in-store discounts, and of course, airline

00:01:03.551 --> 00:01:04.551
miles.

00:01:04.551 --> 00:01:09.820
With all that generosity, how on earth did
credit card companies manage to post $163

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.060
billion in profit in 2016 alone???

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.460
Turns out credit card companies make their
money in a few different ways.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.440
They charge merchants a fee for processing
your payment, which is often passed on to

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.060
the consumer in the form of higher prices.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:27.090
And they sell user data to marketers, so you
might get hit with more advertising.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:32.350
It’s hard to quantify exactly how much these
factors affect you, but there are more direct

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:37.590
costs to being a credit card user.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:41.440
Most Credit card companies have fees attached
— especially if they’re an airline or

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.030
other rewards card.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:48.740
Missing a payment or over-drafting will also
incur late fees or cash advance fees.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:55.659
One study showed an annual card-holder fee
range of $25 - $550 for cards linked to a

00:01:55.659 --> 00:02:00.200
rewards program, with the average annual fee
of $147.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.800
You might still say you got a 'good deal'
on the flight, but if you pay $147 for a free

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.880
thing… it’s not free.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:08.880
Just sayin’.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.760
And simply paying that fee doesn’t guarantee
you’ll actually receive any benefit.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:18.010
A Bankrate.com study found that 31% of airline
miles never get redeemed at all!

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:22.280
That’s at least in part because airlines
make it increasingly difficult to actually

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.909
cash in on your benefits.

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:28.590
It’s such a pain, in fact, that services
have started popping up like BookingGuru that

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:36.829
will help you pay for a flight with your saved
up miles… for a $200 service charge!

00:02:36.829 --> 00:02:40.290
When you opened your first credit card back
in college, you probably told yourself that

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.680
you’d be responsible and always pay off
your full balance every month.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.980
I mean, who would PLAN on paying the U.S.
average interest rate of 17%?

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:49.680
That would be crazy!

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.749
Well, easier said than done.

00:02:51.749 --> 00:02:56.169
The New York Times reports that 60% of credit
card holders don’t pay off the balance they

00:02:56.169 --> 00:02:57.939
accrue each month.

00:02:57.939 --> 00:03:02.200
Even the most conscientious of credit users
can have a car break down a week before pay

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.189
day, or an unexpected trip to the vet for
poor Mr. Fluffles.

00:03:05.189 --> 00:03:06.189
The aggressive interest and late-fees charged
to credit card holders have helped create

00:03:06.189 --> 00:03:07.189
a wide-spread cycle of debt that many families
spend decades trying to escape.

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:08.189
Credit card companies are incentivized to
keep you carrying a balance as long as possible.

00:03:08.189 --> 00:03:10.191
And even if you *never* pay interest on your
cards, are you really getting the better of

00:03:10.191 --> 00:03:11.590
the credit card company?

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.779
Or other credit card users?

00:03:13.779 --> 00:03:17.769
The company can afford to let you “beat
the system” only because they know that

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:21.849
other, less perfect customers will more than
make up the difference.

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:26.700
So you might say that your cousin or friend
who struggles with credit card debt is helping

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.309
pay for your trip.

00:03:31.309 --> 00:03:35.989
Even if you can avoid fees and interest, credit
cards still might be hurting your ability

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:38.670
to save money or pay off debts.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:39.670
How?

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.680
By subconsciously affecting your spending
habits.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.469
A study by Carnegie Mellon found that spending
cash activated the areas of the brain associated

00:03:48.469 --> 00:03:49.739
with pain.

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:51.159
Swiping a card?

00:03:51.159 --> 00:03:52.159
Not so much.

00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:57.219
A landmark study by MIT found that on average,
subjects using credit cards were willing to

00:03:57.219 --> 00:04:02.180
pay almost twice as much for sports tickets
than those using cash.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.480
According to the Carnegie Mellon researchers,
"The nature of credit cards ensures that your

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.769
brain is anaesthetized against the pain of
payment.”

00:04:10.769 --> 00:04:16.010
And rewards like airline miles are specifically
intended to encourage you to use your card

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:17.010
more often.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:21.500
The thought of earning a few more of those
sweet, sweet points might be what pushes you

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:26.229
over the edge to go ahead and buy an extra
pair of sunglasses or splurge on a fancier

00:04:26.229 --> 00:04:27.449
hotel.

00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:31.560
Even if you don’t wind up spending twice
as much, imagine how much you could save if

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.360
you spent just 5 or 10% less a year by using
cash!

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.120
That could seriously add up.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:42.150
When you take these less obvious costs into
account, you wonder whether it’s a better

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:46.060
idea to just buy a $750 plane ticket outright.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:49.931
Which is not to say that you shouldn’t cash
in your rewards if you have them, or that

00:04:49.931 --> 00:04:53.699
anyone should or even could forego credit
cards altogether.

00:04:53.699 --> 00:04:56.790
Credit cards aren’t objectively good…
or bad.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:01.930
They’re a financial tool like any other
that has costs and benefits, risks and rewards.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:08.010
But it’s in these companies best interests
to emphasize the rewards, and downplay the

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:09.010
risks.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:13.140
And it’s in your best interests to take
a step back and look at the whole picture.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.980
And that’s our two cents!

